1
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:06,278
“My Dear Mr Pip:-

2
00:00:06,278 --> 00:00:11,278
“I write this by request of Mr. Gargery, for to let you know that he is going to London in

3
00:00:11,278 --> 00:00:16,919
company with Mr. Wopsle and would be glad if agreeable to be allowed to see you. He would

4
00:00:16,919 --> 00:00:23,599
call at Barnard’s Hotel Tuesday morning at nine o’clock, when if not agreeable please leave word.

5
00:00:23,599 --> 00:00:28,719
Your poor sister is much the same as when you left. We talk of you in the kitchen every night,

6
00:00:28,719 --> 00:00:33,719
and wonder what you are saying and doing. If now considered in the light of a liberty,

7
00:00:33,719 --> 00:00:38,840
excuse it for the love of poor old days. No more, dear Mr. Pip, from

8
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:44,118
“Your ever obliged, and affectionate servant, “Biddy.”

9
00:00:44,118 --> 00:00:51,317
“P.S. He wishes me most particular to write what larks. He says you will understand. I hope and

10
00:00:51,317 --> 00:00:56,919
do not doubt it will be agreeable to see him, even though a gentleman, for you had ever a good heart,

11
00:00:56,919 --> 00:01:01,599
and he is a worthy, worthy man. I have read him all, excepting only the last

12
00:01:01,599 --> 00:01:08,520
little sentence, and he wishes me most particular to write again what larks.”

13
00:01:08,520 --> 00:01:13,199
I received this letter by the post on Monday morning, and therefore its appointment was for

14
00:01:13,199 --> 00:01:19,519
next day. Let me confess exactly with what feelings I looked forward to Joe’s coming.

15
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:27,398
Not with pleasure, though I was bound to him by so many ties; no; with considerable disturbance, some

16
00:01:27,398 --> 00:01:34,797
mortification, and a keen sense of incongruity. If I could have kept him away by paying money,

17
00:01:34,798 --> 00:01:38,638
I certainly would have paid money. My greatest reassurance was that he was

18
00:01:38,638 --> 00:01:44,039
coming to Barnard’s Inn, not to Hammersmith, and consequently would not fall in Bentley

19
00:01:44,039 --> 00:01:49,159
Drummle’s way. I had little objection to his being seen by Herbert or his father,

20
00:01:49,159 --> 00:01:53,558
for both of whom I had a respect; but I had the sharpest sensitiveness as to his

21
00:01:53,559 --> 00:02:01,000
being seen by Drummle, whom I held in contempt. So, throughout life, our worst weaknesses and

22
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,039
meannesses are usually committed for the sake of the people whom we most despise.

23
00:02:06,039 --> 00:02:11,000
I had begun to be always decorating the chambers in some quite unnecessary and inappropriate way

24
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,599
or other, and very expensive those wrestles with Barnard proved to be. By this time,

25
00:02:17,599 --> 00:02:21,879
the rooms were vastly different from what I had found them, and I enjoyed the honour of

26
00:02:21,879 --> 00:02:27,559
occupying a few prominent pages in the books of a neighbouring upholsterer. I had got on

27
00:02:27,559 --> 00:02:34,359
so fast of late, that I had even started a boy in boots,—top boots,—in bondage and slavery to whom

28
00:02:34,359 --> 00:02:40,159
I might have been said to pass my days. For, after I had made the monster (out of the refuse

29
00:02:40,159 --> 00:02:46,398
of my washerwoman’s family), and had clothed him with a blue coat, canary waistcoat, white cravat,

30
00:02:46,398 --> 00:02:51,477
creamy breeches, and the boots already mentioned, I had to find him a little to do and a great deal

31
00:02:51,478 --> 00:02:58,759
to eat; and with both of those horrible requirements he haunted my existence.

32
00:02:58,759 --> 00:03:03,798
This avenging phantom was ordered to be on duty at eight on Tuesday morning in the hall,

33
00:03:03,798 --> 00:03:08,919
(it was two feet square, as charged for floorcloth,) and Herbert suggested certain

34
00:03:08,919 --> 00:03:14,199
things for breakfast that he thought Joe would like. While I felt sincerely obliged to him for

35
00:03:14,199 --> 00:03:21,438
being so interested and considerate, I had an odd half-provoked sense of suspicion upon me,

36
00:03:21,438 --> 00:03:27,558
that if Joe had been coming to see him, he wouldn’t have been quite so brisk about it.

37
00:03:27,559 --> 00:03:31,599
However, I came into town on the Monday night to be ready for Joe, and I got up early in

38
00:03:31,599 --> 00:03:35,759
the morning, and caused the sitting-room and breakfast-table to assume their most splendid

39
00:03:35,759 --> 00:03:42,000
appearance. Unfortunately the morning was drizzly, and an angel could not have concealed the fact

40
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:49,599
that Barnard was shedding sooty tears outside the window, like some weak giant of a Sweep.

41
00:03:49,599 --> 00:03:54,879
As the time approached I should have liked to run away, but the Avenger pursuant to orders

42
00:03:54,879 --> 00:04:01,359
was in the hall, and presently I heard Joe on the staircase. I knew it was Joe, by his

43
00:04:01,359 --> 00:04:07,879
clumsy manner of coming upstairs,—his state boots being always too big for him,—and by the time it

44
00:04:07,879 --> 00:04:14,240
took him to read the names on the other floors in the course of his ascent. When at last he stopped

45
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:19,637
outside our door, I could hear his finger tracing over the painted letters of my name,

46
00:04:19,638 --> 00:04:25,958
and I afterwards distinctly heard him breathing in at the keyhole. Finally he gave a faint single

47
00:04:25,958 --> 00:04:34,118
rap, and Pepper—such was the compromising name of the avenging boy—announced “Mr. Gargery!”

48
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:37,317
I thought he never would have done wiping his feet,

49
00:04:37,317 --> 00:04:42,199
and that I must have gone out to lift him off the mat, but at last he came in.

50
00:04:42,199 --> 00:04:44,598
“Joe, how are you, Joe?”

51
00:04:44,599 --> 00:04:48,240
“Pip, how AIR you, Pip?”

52
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:53,759
With his good honest face all glowing and shining, and his hat put down on the floor between us, he

53
00:04:53,759 --> 00:05:00,199
caught both my hands and worked them straight up and down, as if I had been the last-patented Pump.

54
00:05:00,199 --> 00:05:03,798
“I am glad to see you, Joe. Give me your hat.”

55
00:05:03,798 --> 00:05:06,840
But Joe, taking it up carefully with both hands,

56
00:05:06,840 --> 00:05:12,199
like a bird’s-nest with eggs in it, wouldn’t hear of parting with that piece of property,

57
00:05:12,199 --> 00:05:17,080
and persisted in standing talking over it in a most uncomfortable way.

58
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:23,000
“Which you have that growed,” said Joe, “and that swelled, and that gentle-folked;” Joe considered a

59
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:30,398
little before he discovered this word; “as to be sure you are a honour to your king and country.”

60
00:05:30,398 --> 00:05:34,039
“And you, Joe, look wonderfully well.” “Thank God,” said Joe,

61
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:40,840
“I’m ekerval to most. And your sister, she’s no worse than she were. And Biddy, she’s ever right

62
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:49,919
and ready. And all friends is no backerder, if not no forarder. ’Ceptin Wopsle; he’s had a drop.”

63
00:05:49,919 --> 00:05:55,559
All this time (still with both hands taking great care of the bird’s-nest), Joe was rolling

64
00:05:55,559 --> 00:06:02,000
his eyes round and round the room, and round and round the flowered pattern of my dressing-gown.

65
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,278
“Had a drop, Joe?”

66
00:06:04,278 --> 00:06:09,477
“Why yes,” said Joe, lowering his voice, “he’s left the Church and went into the

67
00:06:09,478 --> 00:06:13,520
playacting. Which the playacting have likeways brought him to London along with

68
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:19,438
me. And his wish were,” said Joe, getting the bird’s-nest under his left arm for the moment,

69
00:06:19,438 --> 00:06:25,398
and groping in it for an egg with his right; “if no offence, as I would ’and you that.”

70
00:06:25,398 --> 00:06:30,238
I took what Joe gave me, and found it to be the crumpled play-bill of a small metropolitan

71
00:06:30,238 --> 00:06:36,840
theatre, announcing the first appearance, in that very week, of “the celebrated Provincial

72
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:41,879
Amateur of Roscian renown, whose unique performance in the highest tragic walk

73
00:06:41,879 --> 00:06:49,080
of our National Bard has lately occasioned so great a sensation in local dramatic circles.”

74
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:52,278
“Were you at his performance, Joe?” I inquired.

75
00:06:52,278 --> 00:06:56,919
“I were,” said Joe, with emphasis and solemnity.

76
00:06:56,919 --> 00:06:59,317
“Was there a great sensation?”

77
00:06:59,317 --> 00:07:06,598
“Why,” said Joe, “yes, there certainly were a peck of orange-peel. Partickler when he see the ghost.

78
00:07:06,599 --> 00:07:11,759
Though I put it to yourself, sir, whether it were calc’lated to keep a man up to his work with a

79
00:07:11,759 --> 00:07:17,798
good hart, to be continiwally cutting in betwixt him and the Ghost with “Amen!” A man may have had

80
00:07:17,798 --> 00:07:23,680
a misfortun’ and been in the Church,” said Joe, lowering his voice to an argumentative and feeling

81
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:28,638
tone, “but that is no reason why you should put him out at such a time. Which I meantersay,

82
00:07:29,599 --> 00:07:34,039
if the ghost of a man’s own father cannot be allowed to claim his attention, what can,

83
00:07:34,039 --> 00:07:39,039
Sir? Still more, when his mourning ’at is unfortunately made so small as that

84
00:07:39,039 --> 00:07:43,879
the weight of the black feathers brings it off, try to keep it on how you may.”

85
00:07:43,879 --> 00:07:48,118
A ghost-seeing effect in Joe’s own countenance informed me that Herbert

86
00:07:48,118 --> 00:07:53,919
had entered the room. So, I presented Joe to Herbert, who held out his hand;

87
00:07:53,919 --> 00:07:58,159
but Joe backed from it, and held on by the bird’s-nest.

88
00:07:58,159 --> 00:08:05,199
“Your servant, Sir,” said Joe, “which I hope as you and Pip”—here his eye fell on the Avenger,

89
00:08:05,199 --> 00:08:10,278
who was putting some toast on table, and so plainly denoted an intention to make that young

90
00:08:10,278 --> 00:08:16,558
gentleman one of the family, that I frowned it down and confused him more—“I meantersay,

91
00:08:16,559 --> 00:08:22,039
you two gentlemen,—which I hope as you get your elths in this close spot? For

92
00:08:22,039 --> 00:08:28,278
the present may be a werry good inn, according to London opinions,” said Joe, confidentially,

93
00:08:28,278 --> 00:08:34,317
“and I believe its character do stand it; but I wouldn’t keep a pig in it myself,—not in the

94
00:08:34,317 --> 00:08:40,118
case that I wished him to fatten wholesome and to eat with a meller flavour on him.”

95
00:08:40,118 --> 00:08:44,278
Having borne this flattering testimony to the merits of our dwelling-place, and having

96
00:08:44,278 --> 00:08:50,317
incidentally shown this tendency to call me “sir,” Joe, being invited to sit down to table,

97
00:08:50,317 --> 00:08:55,438
looked all round the room for a suitable spot on which to deposit his hat,—as if it were only on

98
00:08:55,438 --> 00:09:01,718
some very few rare substances in nature that it could find a resting place,—and ultimately stood

99
00:09:01,719 --> 00:09:08,520
it on an extreme corner of the chimney-piece, from which it ever afterwards fell off at intervals.

100
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:14,958
“Do you take tea, or coffee, Mr. Gargery?” asked Herbert, who always presided of a morning.

101
00:09:14,958 --> 00:09:19,238
“Thankee, Sir,” said Joe, stiff from head to foot,

102
00:09:19,238 --> 00:09:22,840
“I’ll take whichever is most agreeable to yourself.”

103
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:24,798
“What do you say to coffee?”

104
00:09:24,798 --> 00:09:30,159
“Thankee, Sir,” returned Joe, evidently dispirited by the proposal,

105
00:09:30,159 --> 00:09:33,199
“since you are so kind as make chice of coffee,

106
00:09:33,199 --> 00:09:38,519
I will not run contrairy to your own opinions. But don’t you never find it a little ’eating?”

107
00:09:38,520 --> 00:09:41,958
“Say tea then,” said Herbert, pouring it out.

108
00:09:41,958 --> 00:09:47,238
Here Joe’s hat tumbled off the mantel-piece, and he started out of his chair and picked it up,

109
00:09:47,238 --> 00:09:51,359
and fitted it to the same exact spot. As if it were an absolute

110
00:09:51,359 --> 00:09:55,477
point of good breeding that it should tumble off again soon.

111
00:09:55,478 --> 00:09:58,398
“When did you come to town, Mr. Gargery?”

112
00:09:58,398 --> 00:10:03,000
“Were it yesterday afternoon?” said Joe, after coughing behind his hand, as if he

113
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:10,478
had had time to catch the whooping-cough since he came. “No it were not. Yes it were. Yes. It

114
00:10:10,478 --> 00:10:17,398
were yesterday afternoon” (with an appearance of mingled wisdom, relief, and strict impartiality).

115
00:10:17,398 --> 00:10:20,039
“Have you seen anything of London yet?”

116
00:10:20,039 --> 00:10:25,359
“Why, yes, Sir,” said Joe, “me and Wopsle went off straight to look at the Blacking Ware’us. But we

117
00:10:25,359 --> 00:10:29,359
didn’t find that it come up to its likeness in the red bills at the shop doors; which I

118
00:10:29,359 --> 00:10:36,679
meantersay,” added Joe, in an explanatory manner, “as it is there drawd too architectooralooral.”

119
00:10:36,680 --> 00:10:41,199
I really believe Joe would have prolonged this word (mightily expressive to my mind

120
00:10:41,199 --> 00:10:47,840
of some architecture that I know) into a perfect Chorus, but for his attention being providentially

121
00:10:47,840 --> 00:10:54,919
attracted by his hat, which was toppling. Indeed, it demanded from him a constant attention, and a

122
00:10:54,919 --> 00:11:01,879
quickness of eye and hand, very like that exacted by wicket-keeping. He made extraordinary play with

123
00:11:01,879 --> 00:11:09,638
it, and showed the greatest skill; now, rushing at it and catching it neatly as it dropped; now,

124
00:11:09,638 --> 00:11:15,719
merely stopping it midway, beating it up, and humouring it in various parts of the room and

125
00:11:15,719 --> 00:11:22,000
against a good deal of the pattern of the paper on the wall, before he felt it safe to close with it;

126
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:28,599
finally splashing it into the slop-basin, where I took the liberty of laying hands upon it.

127
00:11:28,599 --> 00:11:34,840
As to his shirt-collar, and his coat-collar, they were perplexing to reflect upon,—insoluble

128
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:41,359
mysteries both. Why should a man scrape himself to that extent, before he could consider himself full

129
00:11:41,359 --> 00:11:48,039
dressed? Why should he suppose it necessary to be purified by suffering for his holiday clothes?

130
00:11:48,039 --> 00:11:53,118
Then he fell into such unaccountable fits of meditation, with his fork midway between his

131
00:11:53,118 --> 00:11:59,118
plate and his mouth; had his eyes attracted in such strange directions; was afflicted

132
00:11:59,118 --> 00:12:05,680
with such remarkable coughs; sat so far from the table, and dropped so much more than he ate, and

133
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:12,520
pretended that he hadn’t dropped it; that I was heartily glad when Herbert left us for the City.

134
00:12:12,520 --> 00:12:17,680
I had neither the good sense nor the good feeling to know that this was all my fault,

135
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:22,958
and that if I had been easier with Joe, Joe would have been easier with me. I felt impatient

136
00:12:22,958 --> 00:12:29,159
of him and out of temper with him; in which condition he heaped coals of fire on my head.

137
00:12:29,159 --> 00:12:32,798
“Us two being now alone, sir,”—began Joe.

138
00:12:32,798 --> 00:12:38,080
“Joe,” I interrupted, pettishly, “how can you call me, sir?”

139
00:12:38,080 --> 00:12:41,278
Joe looked at me for a single instant with something faintly

140
00:12:41,278 --> 00:12:47,238
like reproach. Utterly preposterous as his cravat was, and as his collars were,

141
00:12:47,238 --> 00:12:51,477
I was conscious of a sort of dignity in the look.

142
00:12:51,478 --> 00:12:56,520
“Us two being now alone,” resumed Joe, “and me having the intentions and abilities to

143
00:12:56,520 --> 00:13:02,159
stay not many minutes more, I will now conclude—leastways begin—to mention what

144
00:13:02,159 --> 00:13:07,000
have led to my having had the present honour. For was it not,” said Joe,

145
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:12,638
with his old air of lucid exposition, “that my only wish were to be useful to you,

146
00:13:12,638 --> 00:13:18,879
I should not have had the honour of breaking wittles in the company and abode of gentlemen.”

147
00:13:18,879 --> 00:13:24,719
I was so unwilling to see the look again, that I made no remonstrance against this tone.

148
00:13:24,719 --> 00:13:30,278
“Well, sir,” pursued Joe, “this is how it were. I were at the Bargemen t’other night,

149
00:13:30,278 --> 00:13:33,840
Pip;”—whenever he subsided into affection,

150
00:13:33,840 --> 00:13:39,840
he called me Pip, and whenever he relapsed into politeness he called me sir; “when there come up

151
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:46,840
in his shay-cart, Pumblechook. Which that same identical,” said Joe, going down a new track,

152
00:13:46,840 --> 00:13:52,398
“do comb my ’air the wrong way sometimes, awful, by giving out up and down town as it were him

153
00:13:52,398 --> 00:13:57,238
which ever had your infant companionation and were looked upon as a playfellow by yourself.”

154
00:13:57,238 --> 00:14:00,598
“Nonsense. It was you, Joe.”

155
00:14:00,599 --> 00:14:05,278
“Which I fully believed it were, Pip,” said Joe, slightly tossing his head, “though it signify

156
00:14:05,278 --> 00:14:11,359
little now, sir. Well, Pip; this same identical, which his manners is given to blusterous, come

157
00:14:11,359 --> 00:14:17,039
to me at the Bargemen (wot a pipe and a pint of beer do give refreshment to the workingman, sir,

158
00:14:17,039 --> 00:14:30,663
and do not over stimilate), and his word were, ‘Joseph, Miss Havisham she wish to speak to you.’”

159
00:14:30,663 --> 00:14:30,687
“Miss Havisham, Joe?”

160
00:14:30,687 --> 00:14:33,439
“‘She wish,’ were Pumblechook’s word, ‘to speak to you.’” Joe sat and rolled his eyes at the ceiling.

161
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:37,199
“Yes, Joe? Go on, please.”

162
00:14:37,199 --> 00:14:41,919
“Next day, sir,” said Joe, looking at me as if I were a long way off,

163
00:14:41,919 --> 00:14:46,478
“having cleaned myself, I go and I see Miss A.”

164
00:14:46,478 --> 00:14:49,520
“Miss A., Joe? Miss Havisham?”

165
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:53,317
“Which I say, sir,” replied Joe, with an air of legal formality,

166
00:14:53,317 --> 00:14:58,558
as if he were making his will, “Miss A., or otherways Havisham. Her expression air

167
00:14:58,559 --> 00:15:03,638
then as follering: ‘Mr. Gargery. You air in correspondence with Mr. Pip?’

168
00:15:03,638 --> 00:15:09,599
Having had a letter from you, I were able to say ‘I am.’ (When I married your sister,

169
00:15:09,599 --> 00:15:16,798
sir, I said ‘I will;’ and when I answered your friend, Pip, I said ‘I am.’) ‘Would you tell him,

170
00:15:16,798 --> 00:15:22,438
then,’ said she, ‘that which Estella has come home and would be glad to see him.’”

171
00:15:22,438 --> 00:15:28,958
I felt my face fire up as I looked at Joe. I hope one remote cause of its firing may have been my

172
00:15:28,958 --> 00:15:35,278
consciousness that if I had known his errand, I should have given him more encouragement.

173
00:15:35,278 --> 00:15:40,118
“Biddy,” pursued Joe, “when I got home and asked her fur to write the message to you,

174
00:15:40,118 --> 00:15:46,039
a little hung back. Biddy says, ‘I know he will be very glad to have it by word of mouth, it is

175
00:15:46,039 --> 00:15:53,919
holiday time, you want to see him, go!’ I have now concluded, sir,” said Joe, rising from his chair,

176
00:15:53,919 --> 00:15:59,958
“and, Pip, I wish you ever well and ever prospering to a greater and a greater height.”

177
00:15:59,958 --> 00:16:02,359
“But you are not going now, Joe?”

178
00:16:02,359 --> 00:16:04,398
“Yes I am,” said Joe.

179
00:16:04,398 --> 00:16:06,958
“But you are coming back to dinner, Joe?”

180
00:16:06,958 --> 00:16:09,680
“No I am not,” said Joe.

181
00:16:09,680 --> 00:16:17,278
Our eyes met, and all the “Sir” melted out of that manly heart as he gave me his hand.

182
00:16:17,278 --> 00:16:23,958
“Pip, dear old chap, life is made of ever so many partings welded together, as I may say,

183
00:16:23,958 --> 00:16:28,840
and one man’s a blacksmith, and one’s a whitesmith, and one’s a goldsmith, and one’s

184
00:16:28,840 --> 00:16:35,879
a coppersmith. Diwisions among such must come, and must be met as they come. If there’s been

185
00:16:35,879 --> 00:16:42,840
any fault at all to-day, it’s mine. You and me is not two figures to be together in London; nor yet

186
00:16:42,840 --> 00:16:49,438
anywheres else but what is private, and beknown, and understood among friends. It ain’t that I am

187
00:16:49,438 --> 00:16:55,958
proud, but that I want to be right, as you shall never see me no more in these clothes. I’m wrong

188
00:16:55,958 --> 00:17:02,638
in these clothes. I’m wrong out of the forge, the kitchen, or off th’ meshes. You won’t find

189
00:17:02,638 --> 00:17:08,759
half so much fault in me if you think of me in my forge dress, with my hammer in my hand, or even my

190
00:17:08,759 --> 00:17:14,919
pipe. You won’t find half so much fault in me if, supposing as you should ever wish to see me, you

191
00:17:14,919 --> 00:17:19,478
come and put your head in at the forge window and see Joe the blacksmith, there, at the old anvil,

192
00:17:19,478 --> 00:17:25,759
in the old burnt apron, sticking to the old work. I’m awful dull, but I hope I’ve beat out something

193
00:17:25,759 --> 00:17:33,438
nigh the rights of this at last. And so GOD bless you, dear old Pip, old chap, GOD bless you!”

194
00:17:33,438 --> 00:17:39,558
I had not been mistaken in my fancy that there was a simple dignity in him. The fashion of his dress

195
00:17:39,559 --> 00:17:45,919
could no more come in its way when he spoke these words than it could come in its way in Heaven. He

